# Riseup Labs – Software & Digital Services Management System

Product Requirements Document (PRD) and Software Development Specification



Development baseline

Internal web application



## 1. Project overview

The Software & Digital Services Management System is an internal web application for Riseup Labs to manage the purchasing, subscriptions, accounts, renewals, payments and expenses of company software and digital services.

The system will track the complete purchasing lifecycle:

Requisition → Approval → Purchase → Payment Recording → Account Registration → Subscription Tracking → Renewal → Reporting

It will also manage the employees, departments, teams, vendors and approval permissions involved in this process.

### Primary objectives

- Maintain a centralized inventory of purchased software and digital services.
- Track when, how and from whom each service was purchased.
- Record subscription plans, billing cycles, actual payments and future renewal costs.
- Track individual accounts purchased under each service.
- Control who can submit, approve and purchase requisitions.
- Restrict sensitive payment-card information to specifically authorized people.
- Generate monthly and annual expenditure reports.
- Maintain an auditable history of system activities.

### Product boundaries

This is not an HR, EMS, ERP or project-management system.

It will not manage attendance, leave, payroll, recruitment, employee performance or daily project tasks. Employee information exists only to support purchasing, approvals, access permissions and accountability.

## 2. User roles and permissions

The system must use configurable role-based access control (RBAC). Permissions should be assignable to roles and, where necessary, individual users.

| Role                    | Responsibilities                                                             |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Super Admin             | Complete system control and configuration                                    |
| System Admin            | Manage users, organization, services and system settings                     |
| Employee                | Submit purchase requisitions and view own requests                           |
| Team Lead               | Review and approve team requisitions                                         |
| Department Lead         | Review and approve department requisitions                                   |
| Operation Manager       | Provide operational approval                                                 |
| Purchaser               | Purchase approved services and record actual purchase details                |
| Finance                 | Review financial records and payment information within assigned permissions |
| Management              | View authorized dashboards and reports                                       |
| Card Information Viewer | View protected card information only when explicitly authorized              |

A user may have multiple roles. Permissions must be checked on the server for every protected action.

## 3. Organization management

The system requires a simple organizational structure.

Company → Department → Team → Employees

### Department management

Administrators can:

- Create departments.
- Edit department names and descriptions.
- Assign a Department Lead or Head.
- Activate or deactivate departments.

### Team management

Administrators can:

- Create teams under departments.
- Assign Team Leads.
- Move teams between departments when authorized.
- Activate or deactivate teams.

### Employee management

Employee records contain only:

- Full name.
- Designation.
- Phone number.
- Email address.
- Department.
- Team.
- Assigned roles.
- Account status.

The system must support assigning employees as Team Leads, Department Leads, Directors and other authorized positions.

### Employee invitation and activation

1. An administrator creates an employee record and sends an invitation.
2. The employee receives an email containing an account activation link.
3. The invitation is valid for 48 hours.
4. If not activated, the invitation becomes expired and appears in the pending-invitations list.
5. Administrators can resend the invitation.
6. After seven days from the original invitation, an unactivated pending account is automatically deleted.
7. Once activated, the employee can log in and reset their own password.

An expired invitation must not create an active login account. Reinvitation must not create duplicate employee records.

