## 9. Subscription and billing management

Every recurring purchase must have a subscription record.

### Supported billing cycles

- Monthly
- Quarterly
- Half-yearly
- Yearly
- One-time
- Custom recurring

### Subscription information

- Service/tool.
- Individual account, if applicable.
- Plan name.
- Billing cycle.
- Purchase date.
- Subscription start date.
- Current subscription amount.
- Next renewal date.
- Expected renewal amount.
- Currency.
- Auto-renewal status, if known.
- Subscription status.
- Related purchase and payment records.

### Renewal calculation

The system calculates the next renewal date based on the purchase or subscription start date and the selected billing cycle.

Examples:

| Billing cycle | Example purchase date | Next renewal         |
| ------------- | --------------------- | -------------------- |
| Monthly       | 15 November 2026      | 15 December 2026     |
| Quarterly     | 15 November 2026      | 15 February 2027     |
| Half-yearly   | 15 November 2026      | 15 May 2027          |
| Yearly        | 15 November 2026      | 15 November 2027     |
| One-time      | 15 November 2026      | No recurring renewal |

Purchasers or authorized administrators may correct the calculated renewal date when the provider's actual billing date differs.

A renewal must create a new payment or renewal record. Previous payments must never be overwritten.

## 10. Payment card management and restricted visibility

Sensitive financial information

Payment-card information must be protected separately from ordinary system records.

Only specifically authorized persons can view payment-card information. Being an administrator, purchaser, finance user or management user must not automatically grant access to card details.

### Card registration

The card registry should store:

- Card name (e.g. AWS Company Card).
- Card network/provider (Visa, Mastercard, etc.).
- Bank name.
- Cardholder or responsible person.
- Last four digits.
- Currency.
- Expiry month and year, if needed.
- Card status.
- Authorized viewers.

The system must never store the full card number, CVV, PIN or card authentication secrets.

### Card visibility permissions

Card visibility must be an independent permission, separate from general purchase and finance permissions.

| User type                      | Card visibility                                       |
| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Employee                       | No access                                             |
| Team Lead                      | No access by default                                  |
| Department Lead                | No access by default                                  |
| Purchaser                      | No access by default                                  |
| Finance                        | No access by default                                  |
| Management                     | No access by default                                  |
| Specifically authorized person | Access according to assigned permission               |
| Super Admin                    | No automatic exception; must be explicitly authorized |

An authorized person can be granted one of the following permissions:

- View card name only: Can identify the payment card used.
- View masked details: Can see card name, bank, network and last four digits.
- Manage card records: Can create, update, deactivate and assign authorized viewers.
- View card usage: Can see which purchases used a card without seeing protected card details.

### Important visibility rules

1. Only explicitly authorized users can open the protected card details.
2. Unauthorized users must not see card details in forms, reports, exports, search results, notifications or API responses.
3. Purchase records should display a neutral label such as `Restricted Card` to unauthorized users.
4. Card access permissions must be configurable by the Super Admin or designated security administrator.
5. Card-detail access must be logged with the viewer, card, date/time and action.
6. Revoking a user's card permission must immediately prevent further access.
7. Card information must be protected using server-side authorization, not merely hidden in the interface.
8. Card exports and reports must follow the same access restrictions.
9. Changes to card permissions and card records must be audited.
10. Any emergency access mechanism must be explicitly configured, justified and logged.

Important distinction: A user may be allowed to record that a card was used for a purchase without being allowed to see the card's bank, name or last four digits. These must be separate permissions.

