| service_accounts      | Purchased accounts under services          |
| subscriptions         | Subscription details and billing cycle     |
| subscription_renewals | Renewal history and future renewal records |
| payments              | Actual payment records                     |
| payment_cards         | Restricted company card records            |
| card_authorized_users | Users allowed to access card information   |
| card_access_logs      | Card detail access history                 |
| invoices              | Invoice and receipt references             |
| notifications         | In-app notification records                |
| notification_rules    | Notification schedules and settings        |
| audit_logs            | System activity history                    |
| report_archives       | Generated monthly and annual reports       |

### Important database relationships

- One service can have many purchased accounts.
- One service can have many purchases.
- One purchase can create one or more account records.
- One account can have subscription history over time.
- One subscription can have many renewal and payment records.
- One vendor can be associated with many purchases.
- One payment card can be associated with many payments.
- One requisition can have multiple approval stages.
- One requisition can be associated with its resulting purchase records.

Use foreign keys, unique identifiers, appropriate indexes and soft deletion where historical references must remain available.

## 19. Technical architecture

The following stack is recommended, consistent with the proposed application requirements.

| Layer                   | Technology                |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Backend                 | Laravel 12 / PHP 8.3+     |
| Frontend                | Vue 3                     |
| Application integration | Inertia.js                |
| Database                | PostgreSQL                |
| UI styling              | Tailwind CSS              |
| Authentication          | Laravel Authentication    |
| Authorization           | Laravel Policies and RBAC |
| Background processing   | Redis and Laravel Queues  |
| Scheduled tasks         | Laravel Scheduler         |
| File storage            | Private object storage    |
| Web server              | Nginx                     |
| Deployment              | Linux and Docker          |

### Architecture principles

- Build a modular web application rather than a collection of unnecessary microservices.
- Separate business logic into clear modules.
- Enforce permissions on the backend.
- Store invoices and receipts in private storage.
- Use queued jobs for emails, scheduled reminders and report generation.
- Use database transactions for approvals and purchase completion.
- Keep payment-card access authorization independent from general application roles.
- Maintain backups and audit history.

### Security requirements

- HTTPS for all application traffic.
- Secure password hashing.
- Session protection and logout controls.
- Optional MFA, with stronger verification for sensitive operations.
- CSRF protection and input validation.
- Rate limiting for login, invitations and password recovery.
- Server-side authorization on all protected endpoints.
- Secure handling of uploaded invoices and documents.
- Encrypted backups and protected secrets.
- No sensitive credentials or card data in application logs.

Card data security: Store only the permitted card metadata and last four digits. The system is not a payment-card processor and must not store full card numbers, CVV or PIN. Encrypt protected metadata at rest and restrict its decryption to the authorized card-information service or application boundary. A database-only compromise should not expose protected card details.

## 20. Key application workflows

### A. Purchase request to completed purchase

1. Employee logs in.
2. Employee creates a requisition.
3. Employee selects a service/tool or proposes a new one.
4. Employee provides the requested plan, billing cycle and estimated amount.
5. System identifies the applicable approval workflow.
6. Assigned approvers receive notifications.
7. Approvers complete their stages.
8. Once required approvals are complete, the request becomes approved.
9. Assigned purchaser receives the request.
10. Purchaser completes the purchase and records actual details.
11. System creates or updates the service account and subscription.
12. System calculates the next renewal date.
13. Relevant users receive purchase-completion notifications.
14. All actions are logged.

### B. New service creation

1. Authorized administrator or requester proposes a new service.
2. System checks for an existing matching service.
3. Authorized administrator creates or confirms the catalogue entry.
4. Category and provider are assigned.
5. The service becomes available for future requisitions.

The system should prevent accidental duplicate catalogue entries.

### C. Additional account purchase

1. Employee selects an existing service, such as ChatGPT.
2. Employee requests another account and provides the requested account email and plan details.
3. The requisition goes through approval.
4. Purchaser completes the purchase.
5. Purchaser enters the actual account email, plan, price and billing details.
6. System adds the account to the existing ChatGPT inventory.
7. Subscription and renewal records are created.

### D. Monthly purchase planning

1. Scheduler runs seven days before month-end.
2. System collects approved and expected purchases for the following month.
3. It calculates estimated upcoming expenditure.
4. It sends a summary to designated administrators.
5. Notification delivery is recorded.

### E. Restricted card viewing

1. Authorized security administrator grants card-view permission to a specific person.
2. The user authenticates.
3. The server verifies that the user has the specific card-view permission.
4. If authorized, the user can see the permitted card fields.
5. The system records the access.
6. When permission is revoked, further access is denied immediately.

