### 24.2 The prompt

> Design a modern, clean and professional web application UI for **ESTMS – the Riseup Labs Software & Digital Services Management System**, an internal tool used to request, approve, purchase, track and report on company software and digital services.
>
> **Brand and mood.** Follow the Riseup Labs brand: use the brand primary colour as the single accent colour, a deep neutral for the sidebar and headings, and white cards on a light background. The feel should be calm, confident, trustworthy and technology-forward, like a premium SaaS product, not a dense legacy ERP. Use generous white space, rounded cards, subtle shadows, crisp line icons (one consistent icon set) and small, purposeful animations (150–250 ms) for hover, drawer open and status changes.
>
> **Users.** Employees (submit and track requests), approvers (Team Lead, Department Lead, Operation Manager), Purchasers, Finance, Management, Administrators and Card Information Viewers. Each role should see only the menus and actions it is permitted to use.
>
> **Layout.** A fixed left sidebar (collapsible to icons) with the main menu: Dashboard, Requisitions, Services & Tools, Accounts, Subscriptions, Purchases, Vendors, Payment Cards, Organization, Approval Workflows, Reports, Notifications, Audit Logs, Settings. A top bar with global search (Ctrl/Cmd + K command palette), a "New requisition" primary button, the notification bell with unread count, and the user menu. Breadcrumbs and a page title with a short helper line on every page. Use side drawers for quick view and edit, and full pages for long forms.
>
> **Screens to design (desktop first, fully responsive for tablet and mobile):**
>
> 1. **Login, invitation activation and password reset.** Split layout with brand panel and a simple form. Show clear states for expired invitation and resend.
> 2. **Dashboard.** KPI cards (registered services and tools, purchased accounts, active subscriptions, pending approvals, approved but not purchased, monthly expenditure, annual expenditure, upcoming renewals). Below: monthly expenditure trend chart, expenditure by department and by category (donut or bar), an "Upcoming renewals" list with days remaining, and a "Pending actions for me" panel. Filters for date range, department, team, category, vendor, billing cycle and purchase source in a compact filter bar. Never show card details on the dashboard.
> 3. **Requisition list and detail.** Tabs: All, My requests, Needs my approval. Table with status chips (Draft, Submitted, Pending Approval, Returned, Approved, Rejected, Pending Purchase, Purchased, Cancelled), search, filters and bulk actions. The detail page shows a visual approval timeline (each stage as a step with Required or Optional badge, approver avatar, decision, comment and time), requested versus approved versus actual amount side by side, attachments, and a clear approve / reject / return action bar with a comment box.
> 4. **New requisition form.** A guided, single-page form in logical sections (Service, Plan and billing, Cost, Justification, Attachments) with a live summary card on the right, autosave as Draft, inline validation and a duplicate-service warning.
> 5. **Services & Tools catalogue.** Card and table views, a Type → Category → Subcategory filter tree on the left, provider logo placeholders, status badges and a "Create service" drawer. Service detail page shows its accounts, subscriptions, purchases and spend.
> 6. **Accounts.** Per-service account inventory (for example ChatGPT with its individual accounts), showing account email, plan, renewal date, source (Direct or Vendor), vendor and account status, with a clear separation between account status and subscription payment status.
> 7. **Subscriptions and renewals.** Table plus a calendar view of upcoming renewals, colour-coded by urgency (7, 3, 1 days and due today), renewal amount and responsible person.
> 8. **Purchase completion form (Purchaser).** Pre-filled from the approved requisition, with the requested amount shown read-only beside the actual amount and the difference calculated live. Direct versus Vendor switch that reveals the vendor selector. Payment card selector that shows only a neutral "Restricted Card" label unless the user has card permission. Invoice upload with drag and drop and preview. Auto-calculated next renewal date with an authorised override.
> 9. **Vendors.** Directory with contact details, total purchase amount, related purchases and documents.
> 10. **Payment Cards (restricted area).** Visible only to authorised users. Use a distinct, calm "secure area" treatment with a lock icon, masked values (last four digits only), permission levels (name only, masked details, manage, usage) and an access log. For unauthorised users, show the neutral "Restricted Card" chip everywhere with a tooltip explaining that access is restricted, never a blank or broken field.
> 11. **Organization.** Departments, teams and employees with a tree view, employee table, invitation status (Pending, Expired, Active), resend invitation and role assignment.
> 12. **Approval Workflow Builder.** A visual canvas with a left panel to search employees by name or email and browse by department or team, and draggable role chips (Team Lead, Department Lead, Operation Manager). Stages appear as connected cards on the canvas that can be reordered by drag and drop. Each stage has a Required or Optional toggle (Required shown solid, Optional shown dashed), an end node for the Purchaser or purchasing group, validation messages for invalid or circular flows, a version badge, and Save draft / Activate buttons.
> 13. **Reports.** Monthly and annual report pages with summary tiles, charts, comparison tables (requested versus approved versus actual), export buttons (PDF, Excel, CSV) and a report archive list.
> 14. **Notifications, Audit Logs and Settings.** A notification centre with read and unread states; a filterable audit log table with a before-and-after diff drawer; Settings for roles, permissions, reminders, report period and designated administrators.
>
> **Component library.** Buttons (primary, secondary, ghost, destructive), inputs, selects with search, date pickers, status chips, stepper, tabs, data tables (sticky header, sortable, column chooser, row density toggle, pagination), cards, modals, drawers, toasts, empty states with a friendly illustration and one clear action, skeleton loaders, and error states.
>
> **Ease of use.** Keep the main action on every screen obvious, with a maximum of one primary button per view. Use plain language, helpful empty states, inline help text, keyboard shortcuts, remembered filters and smart defaults. Reduce clicks for common tasks: approve from the list, renew from the renewal row, and add another account from the service page.
>
> **Accessibility.** Meet WCAG 2.2 AA: contrast of at least 4.5:1 for text, visible focus rings, full keyboard navigation, minimum 44 px touch targets on mobile, labels on every input, never rely on colour alone (pair every status colour with text or an icon), support reduced motion, and provide light and dark themes using the same tokens.
>
> **Deliverables.** A design system page (tokens, typography, components), then high-fidelity screens for the 14 areas above in light mode, with dark mode for the Dashboard, Requisition detail and Workflow Builder, plus mobile layouts for Login, Dashboard, New requisition and Approvals.

### 24.3 Implementation notes

- Build with Tailwind CSS tokens that map to the table in 24.1, so a brand update is a configuration change, not a redesign.
- Use Vue 3 components through Inertia.js, with a shared layout, sidebar, data-table and drawer component reused across modules.
- Apply the `Restricted Card` treatment as a single reusable component driven by server-side authorisation, so unauthorised users never receive card data in the page payload.